  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Jaini Bharath Kumar vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 14 July, 2022 Hon'Ble Sri Justice Subba Reddy Satti Criminal Petition No. 4810 Of 2022 Order:",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": ". 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that petitioner is innocent and he is falsely implicated in the crime. Learned counsel submits that even as per the confessional statements, the persons found in the vehicle stated that it was loaded with tobacco products and the names of the persons who are dealing with tobacco products, are shown as accused in the present crime. He submits that the products which are being transported will not fall under the definition of FSS Act and tobacco products are defined under COPT Act. He further submits that the quantity of ganja found in possession of the accused is not commercial quantity, as such bar under Section 37 of the NDPS Act is not attracted and sale or possession of tobacco products is not an offence. He submits that some of the accused against whom similar allegations were made, granted bail, by orders, dated 23.09.2020 and 20.10.2021 passed in Crl.P.Nos.3902 of 2020 and 5702 of 2021 respectively. Hence, he prays to grant bail to the petitioner. 5. Learned Special Assistant Public Prosecutor opposed the bail petition on the ground that investigation is pending. 6. Admittedly possession of tobacco products is not an offence and to attract the alleged offences, there must be some material to show that such products consist of poisonous substances endanger to the life of human being. In the case on hand, there is no material to establish the offences Jaini Bharath Kumar vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 14 July, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/43287670/ 1 punishable under Sections 188, 273, 328 and 420 read with 34 of IPC and Sections 57, 58, 63 of FSS Act, against the petitioner.",
    "petitioner_arguments": ". 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that petitioner is innocent and he is falsely implicated in the crime. Learned counsel submits that even as per the confessional statements, the persons found in the vehicle stated that it was loaded with tobacco products and the names of the persons who are dealing with tobacco products, are shown as accused in the present crime. He submits that the products which are being transported will not fall under the definition of FSS Act and tobacco products are defined under COPT Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "He further submits that the quantity of ganja found in possession of the accused is not commercial quantity, as such bar under Section 37 of the NDPS Act is not attracted and sale or possession of tobacco products is not an offence. He submits that some of the accused against whom similar allegations were made, granted bail, by orders, dated 23.09.2020 and 20.10.2021 passed in Crl.P.Nos.3902 of 2020 and 5702 of 2021 respectively. Hence, he prays to grant bail to the petitioner. 5. Learned Special Assistant Public Prosecutor opposed the bail petition on the ground that investigation is pending. 6. Admittedly possession of tobacco products is not an offence and to attract the alleged offences, there must be some material to show that such products consist of poisonous substances endanger to the life of human being.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In the case on hand, there is no material to establish the offences Jaini Bharath Kumar vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 14 July, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/43287670/ 1 punishable under Sections 188, 273, 328 and 420 read with 34 of IPC and Sections 57, 58, 63 of FSS Act, against the petitioner."
